I found something important in `OriginTrackedResource.equals` (line 149):

```java
OriginTrackedResource other = (OriginTrackedResource) obj;
return this.resource.equals(other) && ObjectUtils.nullSafeEquals(this.origin, other.origin);
```

`this.resource.equals(other)` compares the wrapped `resource` against `other` — the *whole wrapper object* — instead of `other.resource`. Since a plain `Resource` will never `.equals()` an `OriginTrackedResource` wrapper, this looks like a latent bug (two `OriginTrackedResource`s wrapping equal resources would never be equal). The `hashCode()` just below uses `this.resource.hashCode()`, confirming the intent was resource-to-resource comparison. Let me check the test to confirm my reading.

